.TH NESTWG 1 "August 2026" "nestwg" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
nestwg \- expose nested WireGuard VPN chains as one host interface
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nestwg validate
.IR chain
.br
.B nestwg plan
.IR chain
.br
.B nestwg up
.RB [ \-v | \-\-verbose ]
.RB [ --wait " duration]
.RB [ --default-route | --route " CIDR..." | --isolated ]
.IR chain
.br
.B nestwg down
.RB [ \-v | \-\-verbose ]
.IR chain
.br
.B nestwg status
.RI [ chain-name ]
.br
.B nestwg diagnose
.IR chain-name
.br
.B nestwg doctor
.br
.B nestwg recover
.I chain-name
.br
.B nestwg version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nestwg
constructs ordered WireGuard hops and exposes only the innermost interface to
ordinary host applications. Each inner VPN connection is forced through the
previous hop. Internal network namespaces preserve the nested data path but
are not part of the normal user interface.
.PP
The chain document orders hops from the outermost entry to the innermost exit.
Endpoint hostnames are resolved and pinned before privileged network changes.
Plans and runtime state do not contain private or preshared keys.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B validate
Strictly parse the chain and referenced WireGuard files without changing the
host.
.TP
.B plan
Print the executable JSON plan, including host-routing intent, pinned
endpoints, interface addresses, allowed routes, implementation resources, and
calculated MTUs.
.TP
.B up
Create the nested chain, initiate its handshakes, expose the final WireGuard
interface according to the configured host-routing mode, and return to the
invoking shell. By default every hop must handshake within ten seconds. Set
.B --wait 0
to skip readiness waiting.
.PP
.B --default-route
overrides the chain with full host routing for every address family covered by
the final peer's default AllowedIPs. A dedicated policy table preserves the
marked outer WireGuard socket's physical route. An OUTPUT kill switch rejects
unmarked non-local traffic that would bypass the visible VPN device.
.PP
Repeated
.B --route CIDR
options override the chain with selected destination routes. Each route has a
lower-priority unreachable alternative to prevent fallback after interface
loss.
.PP
.B --isolated
constructs the chain without exposing it to host traffic. This mode is for
diagnostics and integration work. Routing overrides are mutually exclusive.
.PP
.B \-v, \-\-verbose
prints lifecycle progress. Normal successful output is one concise line.
.TP
.B down
Restore host DNS and routing, return the final interface to its internal
namespace, and remove the chain in reverse order. Cleanup is retryable from
the recorded recovery state. The argument may be an active chain name or the
same YAML path passed to
.BR up .
.TP
.B status
Show active chains, live handshake and transfer information, the host-visible
device, routing mode, and protected routes.
.TP
.B diagnose
Check runtime construction, every hop handshake, the host device, active
routes, policy rules, and mode-specific leak protection.
.TP
.B doctor
Check privileges, netlink access, kernel WireGuard support,
iptables/ip6tables, policy-routing sysctls, and runtime-state security.
.TP
.B recover
Remove strictly named, process-free orphan namespaces after a chain state file
has been lost. Recovery refuses active namespaces and chains that still have a
state record.
.SH HOST ROUTING
The chain's
.B spec.hostRouting.mode
is
.BR default ,
.BR selected ,
or
.BR isolated .
Selected mode requires a non-empty routes list. Every host route and configured
DNS resolver must be covered by the final peer's AllowedIPs. Selected routes
affect all host processes by destination; process, user, domain, and port
selection are not implemented.
.PP
When
.B spec.dns
is non-empty, NestWG configures the host-visible interface through resolvconf
or systemd-resolved's resolvectl and reverts it during down.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/nestwg/<chain>.yaml
Conventional configuration path used when a command receives a bare chain
name. Explicit relative and absolute YAML paths are also accepted.
.TP
.I /run/nestwg
Root-owned mode-0700 locks, recovery state, and resolver input files.
.TP
.I /run/netns/nwg-<chain>-*
Named internal network namespaces owned by a chain.
.SH SECURITY
WireGuard configuration files must not be accessible by group or other users.
Hook commands from wg-quick files are ignored and never executed. Application
protocol encryption such as TLS remains necessary; the exit can observe
plaintext application traffic and final destinations.
.PP
NestWG is pre-release software and must not yet be relied upon as a security or
anonymity boundary. See SECURITY.md and docs/threat-model.md.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Commands return zero on success and nonzero on validation, readiness,
privilege, or kernel-operation failure.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR wg (8),
.BR wg-quick (8),
.BR ip-rule (8),
.BR ip-netns (8),
.BR wireguard (8)
